<ToolLayout title="Password Hashing Algorithms Comparison" description="Compare bcrypt, argon2, scrypt, PBKDF2, and why general-purpose hashes like SHA-256 or MD5 should never be used for passwords.">
  <HashingAlgorithmsComparison client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why password hashing needs to be slow</h2>
  <p>General-purpose hash functions like SHA-256 are designed to be fast — exactly the wrong property for password storage, since it makes brute-force attacks cheap. Password hashing algorithms (bcrypt, argon2, scrypt) are deliberately slow and memory-intensive, making large-scale guessing attacks computationally expensive.</p>

  <h2>How to use this reference</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Check which algorithms are currently recommended versus deprecated.</li>
    <li>Choose argon2id for new projects unless your language/framework has a strong existing convention around bcrypt.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hashing passwords with SHA-256 or MD5</strong> — these are fast, general-purpose hashes, unsuitable for passwords regardless of how they're used.</li>
    <li><strong>Rolling your own salting scheme</strong> instead of using a library's built-in handling — proper libraries handle salting correctly by default.</li>
    <li><strong>Using an outdated work factor</strong> — bcrypt/argon2 parameters should be tuned to take a deliberately noticeable amount of time on current hardware, and this needs periodic revisiting as hardware improves.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Choosing a password hashing algorithm for a new authentication system</li>
    <li>Auditing an existing system that may be using an outdated or inappropriate hash function</li>
    <li>Explaining to a team why "just hash it with SHA-256" isn't sufficient for passwords</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Is bcrypt still acceptable in 2026?</strong> Yes, it remains widely used and secure when configured with an adequate work factor, though argon2id is generally considered the stronger modern default for new projects.</p>
</ToolLayout>
